WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:01.680
My name is Joe Cheng.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:03.870
I am the CTO of Posit.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:07.270
Most days I co-lead the Shiny
team with Winston Chang.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.820
Shiny really started
with the request

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:19.380
from one of our users, one of
our earliest users of RStudio IDE.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:22.920
Professor Danny Kaplan asked
us for an interactive way

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:28.050
to present statistical and
math concepts to students,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:29.393
and he knew R really well.

00:00:29.393 --> 00:00:30.310
So he wanted to do it in R.

00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:33.570
So it wasn't
until probably a year

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.830
after he had made this
request that I stumbled

00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:43.860
onto a technology called Meteor
that was pushing this new to me

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:48.360
very alien form of programming
called reactive programming.

00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:52.110
And it took me a while to get
my head around how it actually

00:00:52.110 --> 00:00:55.170
worked and a little digging
through their source code.

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:58.920
But finally, after I
understood what they were doing

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:02.460
and some time had passed where
I guess my brain could marinate in

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:10.320
it, I woke up one morning and
I just had this vision of HTML

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.340
with no JavaScript in it,
just simple markup that

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:16.860
had indicators of what
elements were inputs

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:19.100
and what elements were outputs.

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.970
Combine that with
reactive code written

00:01:22.970 --> 00:01:28.580
in R that would power
that HTML interface

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:31.730
and tie it all together
with a WebSocket connection.

00:01:31.730 --> 00:01:34.610
It really felt like
this could be a way

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:37.790
to build applications that
were interactive enough

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:42.740
to satisfy the needs of not
just Danny, but a lot of Dannys

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:44.360
out there in the R world.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.760
And I think Shiny will always
find its purest expression in R

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:52.700
just because of how
flexible the language is.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:56.360
But it's impossible to
ignore how many people

00:01:56.360 --> 00:02:00.660
could benefit if we bring those
same ideas to Python as well.

00:02:04.540 --> 00:02:06.880
The design of the
language really

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:08.919
prioritizes ease
of understanding

00:02:08.919 --> 00:02:10.690
and ease of reading.

00:02:10.690 --> 00:02:16.600
Whereas, I think in R we tend
to favor a concise expression

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:21.340
of our ideas, and maybe a
certain elegance and symmetry.

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:28.510
And in Python, readability,
understandability, simplicity,

00:02:28.510 --> 00:02:33.260
those are valued more. So
I think, for some people,

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.730
Python is just home.

00:02:34.730 --> 00:02:40.010
I mean, it's famously the second
best language for anything,

00:02:40.010 --> 00:02:41.030
right?

00:02:41.030 --> 00:02:43.020
I think that's the
label that Pythonistas

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.950
probably wear with pride.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.080
And because Python is the second
best language for anything,

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:55.970
it means that if we want to
reach more people with Shiny,

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:59.280
we can reach all those people
for whom Python is useful.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.480
And that's a lot of people.

